<urn:atm:triage:finding/DAEMON-SHUTDOWN-UDS-LEAK-AL11>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "DAEMON-SHUTDOWN-UDS-LEAK-AL11" ;
  triage:title "atm-daemon's process::exit() bypasses UnixSocketPathGuard's Drop cleanup, leaking the UDS socket file on every shutdown" ;
  triage:description "crates/atm-daemon/src/main.rs's main() (lines 13-21 in source, line 12 with std::process::exit) calls `std::process::exit(exit_code)` unconditionally after `run().await` completes, for BOTH the clean-shutdown success path (Ok(()) -> exit 0) and the error path. `std::process::exit` bypasses Rust's normal destructor/drop-glue execution entirely -- it does not run any live value's Drop impl. `UnixSocketPathGuard` (crates/atm-http-runtime/src/unix_socket.rs:224-260) is specifically designed to remove its owned UDS socket inode on Drop (device+inode-matched, so it only removes its own socket, not an unrelated one) -- but because the daemon process always exits via process::exit(), this guard's Drop::drop() never runs, so the socket file at the configured path is never unlinked on shutdown, clean or otherwise. This was reproduced live in production on 2026-08-08: the daemon (LaunchAgent com.atm.daemon.crosshost-smoke) received a clean shutdown request at 03:33:49Z (shutdown_requested -> shutdown_worker -> shutdown_completed all logged cleanly in atm.log.jsonl), but the UDS socket file at ~/.atm/daemon/atm-daemon.sock was left behind. Every subsequent launchd KeepAlive restart attempt crash-looped with 'Unix HTTP socket path is already occupied' (crates/atm-http-runtime/src/unix_socket.rs:69-85's ensure_unix_socket_path_unoccupied refusing to rebind over the stale inode) until launchd's failure throttle disabled the service entirely -- a full daemon outage requiring manual intervention (confirm no live holder via lsof/ps, manually rm the stale socket, launchctl kickstart to force respawn) to recover. RECHECK (QA-AL11-1, 2026-08-08, PR #786 @ 91dad38e): CLOSED. crates/atm-daemon/src/main.rs now returns std::process::ExitCode instead of calling std::process::exit(); independently confirmed the fix is textually and semantically correct (Ok(())=>ExitCode::SUCCESS, Err=>ExitCode::from(replacement_exit_code(&error)), exit-code values 0/1/64/70 all unchanged). This is not merely plausible -- it is Rust's own documented, guaranteed language behavior that returning from main (any non-!  type) runs live destructors before process termination, while std::process::exit() explicitly does not; removing the exit() call mechanically eliminates the described bug's exact mechanism. VALIDATION-COVERAGE CONCERN (raised by team-lead, independently investigated by quality-mgr): the cited proof (cargo test -p atm-http-runtime unix_socket_uses_the_shared_client_router_and_owner_only_endpoint) is real and passes, but is 100% in-process -- it calls running.begin_shutdown().finish().await directly on HttpRuntime within the test's own async task, and never touches main()/ExitCode/process::exit() at all. It would have passed identically before this fix, since it never exercises the buggy code path. This is a genuine validation-specificity gap, not a false alarm. Quality-mgr attempted to construct a true binary-level integration proof (built the release atm-daemon binary, attempted to spawn it as an isolated subprocess with ATM_HOME/HOME pointed at a temp directory) but the daemon's owner-lock singleton resolution did not honor the ATM_HOME override for its production lock path, and further debugging risked disrupting the live crosshost-smoke LaunchAgent daemon (a different, unrelated M5 hardware-smoke effort in progress on this same host) -- declined to proceed further given the risk/reward. Verdict: the code fix's correctness is not in doubt (guaranteed by Rust's own documented semantics, independently confirmed by direct code read), but durable regression protection against a FUTURE reintroduction of process::exit() in this binary is missing. See new finding DAEMON-SHUTDOWN-REGRESSION-TEST-GAP-AL11 for the follow-up, filed as non-blocking." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-al" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "runtime-lifecycle-defect" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "workspace" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
